Creating a context menu in TypeScript can be achieved using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Below is a simple example of how you can implement a context menu that appears when right-clicking on an element in your webpage.

### Step 1: Create the HTML Structure

First, set up the basic HTML structure for your page.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Context Menu Example</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="content" onclick="showContextMenu(event)">Right-click here to show context menu</div>

    <div class="context-menu" style="display:none;">
        <ul>
            <li>Option 1</li>
            <li>Option 2</li>
            <li>Option 3</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 2: Add CSS for Styling

Create a `styles.css` file to style the context menu.

```css
.context-menu {
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #fff;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    list-style-type: none;
    padding: 5px 0;
    margin: 0;
    z-index: 9999;
}

.context-menu li {
    padding: 8px 12px;
    cursor: pointer;
}

.context-menu li:hover {
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
}
```

### Step 3: Implement JavaScript for Functionality

Create a `script.js` file to handle the context menu functionality.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    var content = document.getElementById('content');
    var contextMenu = document.querySelector('.context-menu');

    content.addEventListener('contextmenu', function(event) {
        event.preventDefault(); // Prevent default right-click menu

        contextMenu.style.display = 'block';
        contextMenu.style.left = (event.pageX + 10) + 'px';
        contextMenu.style.top = (event.pageY - 5) + 'px';

        document.addEventListener('click', hideContextMenu);
    });

    function hideContextMenu() {
        contextMenu.style.display = 'none';
        document.removeEventListener('click', hideContextMenu);
    }

    // Add event listeners to menu items
    var options = contextMenu.querySelectorAll('li');
    options.forEach(function(option) {
        option.addEventListener('click', function() {
            alert('You clicked: ' + this.textContent);
            hideContextMenu();
        });
    });
});
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML**: The `div` with the id `content` is set up to trigger the context menu when right-clicked.

2. **CSS**: The `.context-menu` class styles the context menu, positioning it relative to the mouse click and adding some basic styling.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - An event listener is added to the `div` with the id `content` to detect right-click events.
   - The default behavior of the right-click (which displays the browser's context menu) is prevented using `event.preventDefault()`.
   - The context menu is displayed at the position of the mouse click (`event.pageX` and `event.pageY`).
   - An event listener is added to hide the context menu when any part of the page is clicked, except for the context menu itself.
   - Event listeners are added to each menu item to handle user interactions.

This example provides a basic implementation of a context menu in TypeScript. You can expand upon it by adding more options, styling, or functionality as needed.
